---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PhoneDemo from '../../components/PhoneDemo.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Demo"
  description="The Lumen app running in your browser: the same screens, wording and plugin logic as the real app, computed locally."
>
  <div class="hero page-hero">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Demo</p>
    <h1 class="hero-enter">The real app, in your browser</h1>
    <p class="lead hero-enter" style="animation-delay: 160ms">
      This phone is a faithful copy of the Lumen app's screens, running the real logic from
      <a href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-plugins">lumen-plugins</a> compiled to
      JavaScript. Answer the five onboarding questions, or skip them, then open any of the eight
      modules and use its tools.
    </p>
  </div>

  <PhoneDemo />

  <section>
    <div class="notice">
      Nothing you type leaves your device: every result is computed locally in your browser, with no
      network request. Like the app itself, this demo only runs the parts of each module that do not
      need a camera, microphone or motion sensor, since those native bridges do not exist yet (see
      <a href="/docs/introduction/status/">project status</a>). The SOS tool uses the plugin's fake
      message sender and can never send a real message, and the fall and walking tools run on
      simulated sensor readings.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <h2>What is real here</h2>
    <p>
      The screens follow the Flutter app's own code: the same palette, spacing, components and wording
      (the English strings are extracted from <code>lumen-app</code>), the same onboarding rules that
      decide which modules are enabled and which interface mode applies, and the same settings. Every
      tool calls the real plugin classes: <code>ExpiryDateParser</code>, <code>ColorNamer</code> and
      <code>ContrastChecker</code> for Vision; <code>Summarizer</code>, <code>CaptionFormatter</code> and
      <code>AlertPresentationMapper</code> for Hearing; <code>ScamFilter</code>, <code>FallDetector</code>,
      <code>CheckInScheduler</code> and <code>SosController</code> for Safety;
      <code>PairingCodeGenerator</code>, <code>InMemoryCalendarStore</code>,
      <code>DailyStatusTracker</code> and <code>CaregiverNotificationComposer</code> for Family;
      <code>PhraseBoard</code>, <code>PhraseUsageTracker</code> and <code>SentenceBuilder</code> for
      Speech; <code>ItalianSyllableSplitter</code>, <code>SpacedRepetitionScheduler</code> and
      <code>ReadingTimeEstimator</code> for Reading; <code>PersonDirectory</code>,
      <code>ReminderScheduler</code> and <code>RoutineChecklistTracker</code> for Memory; and
      <code>GaitStabilityMonitor</code>, <code>MobilityProfile</code> and <code>RestBreakPlanner</code>
      for Mobility.
    </p>
    <p>
      State lasts for the visit, as in a real session: the flashcard schedule, reminders, people,
      routine checklist, phrase usage and calendar all persist while you move between screens. Use
      <strong>Restart the demo</strong> under the phone to go back to onboarding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <h2>How this demo is built</h2>
    <p>
      A small build tool, <a href="https://github.com/lumen-assistive/lumen-website/tree/main/tools/web-demo">tools/web-demo</a>,
      imports the business logic of all eight modules, pinned to the same <code>lumen-plugins</code> tag
      every other consumer uses, and compiles it with <code>dart compile js</code> into the script
      loaded on this page. The screens themselves are plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript that mirror the
      app's widgets; they never compute a result on their own.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<script is:inline src="/demo/lumen-demo.js" defer></script>
<script is:inline src="/demo/phone.js" defer></script>

<style>
  /* Long class names must wrap on phone widths instead of widening the page. */
  .band code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
